Chapter 8 dives deeper into operationalization. Operationalization is important to the project because it defines exactly how each variable is going to be measured and coded. It makes sure the coding is not left up to any individual coder’s interpretation, so that two coders would know to code a variable the same way. 
In deciding on my topic and research questions, the variables I decided to measure were the number of words in each comment and who the comment is directed at. 
The number of words is surface-level and doesn’t need further definition on how it will be coded. Who the comment is directed at is a variable that will need to be more carefully and strictly defined because it could easily be interpreted differently by two different coders. My criteria for this variable will be similar to the Twitch example in the textbook but adjusted for Youtube and Instagram. A comment would be directed at the creator if it contains the creator’s name or a second-person address such as "you". I would most likely also add a rule for comments greeting the creator, like "hey girl", etc., to be coded as directed at the creator. It would be broadcast to the room if it is a comment not addressed at anyone in particular. Comments mentioning another commenter are directed at another commenter, though it’s uncommon for original youtube or instagram comments to be directed at other commentors because those live in the replies section underneath the original comment, which I won’t be measuring. This did make me consider adding number of replies as another variable, however. Lastly a comment would be coded as unclassifiable if it doesn't fit into any of those three categories.
